# Line endings, so that the published checks pass on a fresh clone on every platform.
#
# SHA256SUMS was computed over the release files as they were written, and the JSON transcripts
# and the CSV tables were written with Windows line endings (Python's csv module writes \r\n).
# Git stores text with Unix endings, so without these lines a clone on Linux or macOS fails
# `sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS` on 63 of its 84 files, and RECOMPUTE.md step 4's regenerated tables
# differ from the checkout by their line endings alone. The last two lines restore the written
# bytes on checkout. They change no stored file.
#
# The first three stop every other sealed file being converted. Git for Windows converts text to
# CRLF on checkout by default (core.autocrlf=true), and with only the last two lines that turned the
# seven SVG figures and the two master tables into bytes SHA256SUMS never saw: 75 of 84 passed. Found
# by the 2026-09-27 review. -text means "check out exactly what is stored"; the later lines win for
# the JSON and CSV files, because a later matching line overrides an earlier one.
02_data/** -text
.zenodo.json -text
CITATION.cff -text
02_data/raw/*.json text eol=crlf
02_data/tables/*.csv text eol=crlf
